From f8c72803d16495daee15a22119a006e4d8cf433f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 12:17:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 026/206] feat(shorebird): CT/FT/DT readers; move
 ShorebirdLinker to dart:: namespace

Two things, both driven by their symbol table.

Namespace correction (fixes Task 1). Their symbols demangle to
dart::ShorebirdLinker, not dart::shorebird::ShorebirdLinker -- the only
things they put in dart::shorebird:: are the WrapperAllocator/WrapperLayout
family, so wrapper.{h,cc} stays where it is and link_info.{h,cc} was already
right. Our guard symbol now matches theirs verbatim:

  guard variable for dart::ShorebirdLinker::shared()::shared_linker

including the static's name, which their guard variable reveals. Verified
6 dart::ShorebirdLinker symbols and 0 dart::shorebird::ShorebirdLinker in
run_vm_tests. This mattered beyond tidiness: a symbol-level check grepping
shorebird::ShorebirdLinker would have matched ours while never being able to
match theirs, reporting success exactly when we diverged.

Readers. ReadClassTableLinkInfo / ReadFieldTableLinkInfo /
ReadDispatchTableLinkInfo each come in two overloads, with the ReadStream*
form as the primary decoder and the const char* form reading the file and
delegating to it -- one decoder, so the production path and the test path
cannot drift apart. All three are count-driven, never read-until-EOF: the
class and field formats put their scalar LAST (num_cids / max_field_id), so a
parser that loops until the buffer is exhausted swallows the trailer as a
phantom entry and then reports a clean full-buffer parse. Read the count, loop
exactly that many times, then read the trailer. ReadString mirrors WriteString
in link_info.cc: length prefix, then each char as a signed int32 varint.

A zero-length file is treated as a read error rather than decoded: no valid
encoding is zero bytes, and ReadStream's bounds checks are ASSERTs that are
compiled out in Release/Product, so decoding an empty buffer would over-read
the allocation instead of failing.

Because both overloads take pointers, a bare nullptr is ambiguous; the tests
cast explicitly and say why.

Scope limit recorded in the test file: these three tests exercise only the
null-path guard, which returns before any decoding happens. They cannot
distinguish a correct decoder from one returning the right count of wrong
entries, nor one that stops early. Task 3's round-trip is the first real test
of the decoder.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

